<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Artemis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Artemision, Temple of Artemis at Ephesus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, the Artemision, was a great sanctuary of the goddess and one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world. The marble temple of the 6th century BCE, partly paid for by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a>, was burned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herostratus">Herostratus</a> in 356 BCE and rebuilt on an even larger scale. It was damaged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> in the 3rd century CE, and only fragments remain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis.jpg" alt="Temple of Artemis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: autor fotografie Zee Prime Original uploader was Zee Prime at cs.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miniaturk_009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
